From 53bf6bd73047dbc7638a6a7cf8535a46f00ebe12 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Velle Sinclair <brncomputerhelp@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 22:29:33 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 46/46] feat(render): a layer or an effect a script shows or
 hides draws accordingly

An image layer wired its passes once, at load. A layer hidden then had no
pass drawing onto the scene, so a script that showed it later (a media
player's settings panel, a drag handle, a day/night controller: 49 layers
in 7 wallpapers start hidden behind a script) drew into its own
framebuffer and never appeared. An effect hidden at load was never built,
and the visibility of one that was did nothing: the passes drew it anyway.
Scripts on an effect's visibility did not run at all.

The passes are now wired again whenever the layer or one of its effects
is shown or hidden: those of a hidden effect are left out of the chain,
each pass reads what the one before it drew, the last draws onto the
scene, and the layer's blending moves to whichever pass that is. Nothing
changes while the visibility does not, so no layer re-wires every frame.

An effect hidden at load is built only when a script or a timeline drives
its visibility, so effects that a user setting hides cost nothing.
The effect's visibility script runs with the effect as thisObject.

